--TEST--
Core\Csrf, Core\SignedCookie and Core\Crypto::seal share one construction and one key type, and no one's output opens as another's — a cookie binds its name and its own domain octet into the additional data — `rule:security/protocol-roster` and `rule:security/algorithm-comes-from-the-key`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Edges. The boundary this case sits on is the one a deployment reaches by
// itself: an application generates one key with `Core\Crypto::generateKey()`
// and uses it for its signed cookies, its CSRF tokens and its own sealed
// messages, because nothing stops it. Without a binding, a signed cookie
// carrying a session identifier would be a valid CSRF token for that session,
// and a token or a sealed message would open as a cookie.
// `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/signed_cookie.rs` owns the cookie's binding.
secret bytes $key = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
array<secret bytes> $ring = [$key];
string $session = "sid-ada";

// A signed cookie of exactly the session identifier, which is the value an
// application most plausibly seals: it is what a session cookie holds.
string $cookie = Core\SignedCookie::seal('session', $session, $ring);
if (Core\Csrf::verify($cookie, $session, $key) == false) {
    echo "a signed cookie is not a token", "\n";
} else {
    echo "a signed cookie passed as a token", "\n";
}

// And the reverse: a token does not open as a cookie under any name.
string $token = Core\Csrf::issue($session, $key);
try {
    Core\SignedCookie::open('session', $token, $ring);
    echo "a token opened as a cookie", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo "a token is not a cookie", "\n";
}

// A message `Core\Crypto::seal` made, written as a cookie, is not one either.
string $sealed = Core\Encoding::toBase64Url(Core\Crypto::seal($session as bytes, $key, Core\Crypto::Cipher::XChaCha20Poly1305));
try {
    Core\SignedCookie::open('session', $sealed, $ring);
    echo "a sealed message opened as a cookie", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo "a sealed message is not a cookie", "\n";
}

// A token is still a token afterwards — nothing above consumed it — and it is
// still bound to its own session and no other.
if (Core\Csrf::verify($token, $session, $key)) {
    echo "and the token still verifies", "\n";
} else {
    echo "the token stopped verifying", "\n";
}
if (Core\Csrf::verify($token, "sid-bob", $key) == false) {
    echo "against its own session only", "\n";
} else {
    echo "it verified against another session", "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
a signed cookie is not a token
a token is not a cookie
a sealed message is not a cookie
and the token still verifies
against its own session only
